---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Field from '../components/Field.astro';

---
<Base title="What is in the language" description="Every construct in Hale, with the kind of evidence behind it: shipped, Linux-only, modeled, reserved or next release.">
  <Field />
  <section class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>What is in the language</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything here is implemented, statically checked and specified unless its row says otherwise: the syntax parses, the checks run
        and the runtime path exists. The <a href="/docs/spec/semantics">specification</a> describes current behaviour, and this page follows it.
      </p>
      <p class="ev-key">
        Rows whose evidence differs are marked. <span class="ev">linux</span>
        is a platform-specific path, <span class="ev">modeled</span> is
        checked as a formal model whose match to the shipped code is
        maintained by hand, and <span class="ev">reserved</span> is named and
        specified but not built. <span class="ev">next release</span> is on the
        compiler's main branch and ships in the next release.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose wide">
      <h2>One construct</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>The locus</b><p>One construct where other languages have class, module, package, actor and service. An app, a cache, a handler and a library are all loci. Composition is a locus inside a locus.</p></li>
        <li><b>Lifecycle</b><p><code>birth</code>, <code>run</code> and <code>dissolve</code>; <code>params &#123; &#125;</code> with defaults; parent and child nesting with deterministic cleanup on dissolve.</p></li>
        <li><b>Perspectives</b><p>Live hot code swap at the cost of a pointer flip. <code>reperspective</code> carries state across and re-points bus edges without dropping messages.</p></li>
        <li><b>Modes</b><p><code>bulk</code>, <code>harmonic</code> and <code>resolution</code> projections over capacity slots, for programs that reshape under load.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The typed bus</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>Topics are declarations</b><p><code>topic T &#123; payload: P; &#125;</code> with wire subjects, routing keys (<code>keyed_by</code>), and per-topic bounds with an explicit backpressure contract (<code>or wait</code>).</p></li>
        <li><b>Traffic is declared</b><p>Peer loci do not reference one another. They communicate through a declared topic, and messages are copies. Subscription and publish are declared surface, so the compiler sees the whole message graph.</p></li>
        <li><b>Topology checked at compile time</b><p><code>hale check</code> rejects payload mismatches and unconditional re-entry inside a locus. It reports orphaned topics, cross-locus cycles and statically unbounded backpressure as advisories. <code>hale verify</code> turns every advisory into a failing gate, so a project chooses its own strictness and the two kinds of finding stay distinct.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Types and data</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>Structural interfaces</b><p>An <code>interface</code> is satisfied by shape. There is no <code>impl I for L</code>.</p></li>
        <li><b>Synthesized forms</b><p><code>@form(vec | hashmap | ring_buffer | lru_cache)</code> generates type-specialized collections; <code>bounded[T; N]</code> gives fixed capacity. No generics on the everyday path.</p></li>
        <li><b>Element chains</b><p><code>xs.filter(it &gt; 2).count()</code> compiles to one loop, allocation-free, with no closure or iterator object.</p></li>
        <li><b>Money and time as types</b><p><code>Decimal</code>, typed timestamps and durations. Inference for <code>let</code>, returns and locus params. f-strings.</p></li>
        <li><b>Units, quantities, identities, ranges</b><p>A program declares units and the equations between them (<code>unit USD = 100 cent;</code>), then integer types counted in them: a <code>quantity</code> such as Money, a <code>point</code> such as Price, a <code>distinct Int</code> that never mixes with another, and an <code>Int &#123; range: 0..256; &#125;</code>. Every conversion is one exact factor; widening is free and narrowing names its rounding. The standard library's time units are declared the same way. New in 0.23.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Failure</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b><code>fallible(E)</code> in the type</b><p>Every call that can fail is addressed at the call site with a disposition: <code>or default</code>, <code>or raise</code>, <code>or wait</code> and the rest. No exceptions, no <code>panic</code>.</p></li>
        <li><b>Failure travels up</b><p>A child's failure goes to its parent, which owns recovery. There is no sideways error channel.</p></li>
        <li><b><code>closure</code> assertions</b><p>Declared invariants, audited by the runtime at phase boundaries.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Effects, claims, constitutions, fleets</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>Effect certificates</b><p>Ten effect classes (<code>syscall</code>, <code>block</code>, <code>time</code>, <code>entropy</code>, <code>alloc</code> and five more). Opt-in assertions such as <code>@no_block</code>, <code>@deterministic</code> and <code>@effects(only: …)</code> are proven transitively over the whole call graph, with numeric budgets (<code>@budget</code>) and per-phase contracts (<code>@phase_effects</code>). Incompleteness fails closed.</p></li>
        <li><b>Claims</b><p>Named sentences over the assembled program graph, declared in <code>main</code>, checked as errors, no runtime cost: <code>forbid reaches</code>, <code>only edges</code>, <code>require</code>, <code>cover</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>bound</code>, interposition with <code>avoiding</code>, lifecycle slices with <code>during</code>. A violation returns a minimal countermodel in your own names.</p></li>
        <li><b>Constitutions</b><p>One claimset adopted by many entry points, composed by union only, bound per deployment target in <code>hale.toml</code>, and proven pairwise by <code>hale check --matrix</code>.</p></li>
        <li><b>Fleets</b><p>Per-application topology artifacts compose into one deployment model. Cross-binary claims get cross-binary witnesses. Only an explicit route creates an edge. Signing and binary attestation carry the certificate to the machine that deploys it.</p></li>
        <li><b>The topology artifact</b><p>Byte-reproducible JSON with source maps, an integrity digest and every claim verdict. Commit it, and diff the architecture in review along with the text.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Memory and runtime</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>Arena per locus</b><p>No garbage collector, no borrow checker, no lifetime annotations. Memory is freed deterministically when a locus dissolves.</p></li>
        <li><b>Model-checked synchronization <span class="ev">modeled</span></b><p>Hand-transcribed models of the runtime's concurrent protocols (the lock-free bus, mailboxes, rings, arenas) are explored with GenMC in CI. The models check the synchronization logic across permitted interleavings. Their match to the production runtime is maintained by hand and is part of the trusted base. <a href="/proof#substrate">What that does and does not establish.</a></p></li>
        <li><b>Static dispatch by default</b><p>Devirtualized bus delivery and calls. Cooperative pools with epoll-backed parking. Pinned threads and <code>replicas = K</code> fan-out declared in <code>main</code>. NUMA-aware arena placement and cache-domain pinning <span class="ev">linux</span>.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Deployment</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>Same source, many shapes</b><p><code>placement &#123; &#125;</code> and <code>bindings &#123; &#125;</code> in <code>main</code> decide threads, cores and transports. A test, one binary or a mesh of binaries is an edit to one block.</p></li>
        <li><b>Transports</b><p>In-process queues, Unix sockets, zero-copy shared-memory rings, and broker adapters written as ordinary loci. Pluggable codecs.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Exposure <span class="ev">next release</span></h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>API surfaces</b><p><code>api NAME &#123; rpc Locus::fn requires: [role]; &#125;</code>, or <code>@rpc</code> on the handler. The compiler checks the rows, folds each surface into a contract digest, and prints the description a caller may see. <code>hale check --api</code> prints the inventory, and <code>--openapi</code>, <code>--json-schema</code> and <code>--mcp</code> print a surface's client forms.</p></li>
        <li><b>Roles</b><p>Declared vocabulary: <code>role manager includes clerk;</code>. A requirement is written once on the row; who holds a role is a source the serve site names, <code>std::api::StaticRoles</code> or any locus satisfying <code>std::api::RoleSource</code>.</p></li>
        <li><b>Serving</b><p><code>api::serve(Surface, transport, as:, receivers:, bound:, on_full:)</code> over <code>unix::Rpc</code>, <code>http::Rpc</code> or <code>mcp::Rpc</code>; a stream is a topic bound to a <code>ws::Hub</code>. A handler may take <code>ctx: std::api::Context</code> for the caller, the request id and the door it came through.</p></li>
        <li><b>Clients</b><p><code>hale describe</code>, <code>call</code>, <code>watch</code>, <code>admin</code> and <code>mcp --app</code> read an exposure's description and speak the contract's wire. The DNA head serves its commands the same way.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Interop</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>C</b><p><code>@ffi("c")</code> bindings with <code>[ffi] csrc</code> compilation, in both directions across the boundary.</p></li>
        <li><b>WebAssembly</b><p>A <code>wasm32</code> target with <code>@export</code> loci and <code>@ffi("js")</code>. The <a href="/playground">playground</a> runs the result in the browser.</p></li>
        <li><b>Packages</b><p><code>hale fetch</code> vendors pinned git dependencies. <a href="/packages">pond</a> is the contributed library collection.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Standard library</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>Path-called, no imports</b><p>Processes, env, filesystem, TCP and UDP with zero-copy readers, TLS, HTTP (router and client), JSON including a streaming builder, metrics, logging with sinks, compression, tar, crypto and hashing, encodings, randomness, strings, regex, sets, time. <code>hale doc --stdlib</code> publishes each function's effect classes from the same registry the checker proves against.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Toolchain</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>One binary</b><p>build, run, check, verify, test, bench, fmt, doc, fetch and fleet, with the language server and the MCP server compiled in, so an editor, a CI job and a coding assistant read the same diagnostic. <a href="/tooling">The toolchain in full.</a></p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Platforms</h2>
      <p>Three different questions, easy to run together: where a prebuilt compiler exists, where one can be built, and what a build can emit.</p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>Prebuilt compiler</b><p>Linux x86-64, Linux ARM64 and macOS on Apple Silicon, on GitHub Releases, selected automatically by the install script.</p></li>
        <li><b>Builds from source</b><p>The above, plus Intel macOS. Needs the LLVM 18 development libraries and <code>clang</code>.</p></li>
        <li><b>Emits</b><p>Native binaries for Linux and macOS, and <code>wasm32</code> objects for the browser. <code>x86_64-pc-windows-msvc</code> is named by <code>hale --list-targets</code> and refused with a precise error before any link step <span class="ev">reserved</span>.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Reserved</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><b>Surface awaiting a workload</b><p><code>async</code> and <code>await</code> keywords, traits with explicit impls, macros, richer form shapes. The runtime frontier is cost: a cheaper call protocol and spawn path. The spec stays the arbiter of what ships.</p></li>
        <li><b>To move a priority</b><p>The fastest signal is a real program that needs it. See <a href="/community">community</a>.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
